what REDS %787: the claim board every ship writes and every ship reads has two shapes, and this tree checks only one. fleet_claim_scan.sh reads CONTENT -- overlap, staleness, expiry -- and is line-oriented and forgiving by construction, which is right for reading and exactly wrong for noticing. Its field rules all guard on if (name != ""), so a field standing above the first claim header is silently dropped, and a record whose header is eaten donates its five fields to the record before it, overwriting them. A hand-resolved rebase conflict did exactly that on 20260916 and --check answered verdict=clear with claims_live=8 over a board holding one corrupted record and one invisible one. Seat a FORM reading beside the content one: every record carries its five fields exactly once, no field stands orphaned, no line runs past a bound, no what contains another record's header, no duplicate claim name, no unknown key, and seat/stamp/epoch/paths each parse. Then make --check itself refuse -- verdict=malformed rather than clear -- because a reader that cannot trust its board must answer so, the way the derived spine refuses a spine that cannot answer rather than calling everything shared.
